P27.content:
To evaluate your game as a center, also notice actions such as:

- boxing out an opponent so someone else can secure the rebound;
- altering a drive without needing to block it;
- preventing a drive simply through your position;
- setting a screen that creates an advantage;
- re-screening with a better angle;
- running to the rim and forcing the defense to collapse;
- sealing and opening a driving lane for another player;
- making a short roll that forces a rotation;
- finding a pass after drawing help;
- protecting the rim without fouling;
- helping and recovering;
- communicating a coverage before the screen;
- keeping an offensive rebound alive;
- creating a second-chance opportunity;
- occupying the right space during a drive.

After the game, you can ask yourself:

- Did I finish defensive possessions?
- Did I box out before looking for the rebound?
- Did I protect the rim with good position?
- Did I commit avoidable fouls trying to block everything?
- Did I set screens that created advantages?
- Did I do something useful after screening?
- Did I run with intention?
- Did I occupy interior spaces well?
- Did I pass when help arrived?
- Did I create second-chance opportunities?
- Did I communicate coverages?

P04.content:
Do not try to develop every creation tool of a point guard and every scoring solution of a shooting guard at the same time.

First build a reliable connection between both functions:

- receive and decide quickly;
- pass and relocate;
- attack an advantage without stopping it;
- take over handling when needed;
- play without the ball when someone else organizes.

The combination works when you can change function without changing the quality of your decisions.

P21.content:
Being able to organize does not mean you should ask for the ball every time someone else has it.

Being able to score does not mean every catch should end in a shot.

This combination helps the team especially when it can:

- initiate an advantage;
- give up the ball on time;
- maintain spacing;
- receive again without stopping the possession;
- create a second advantage if the defense has already shifted.

Being useful with and without the ball makes it easier to share the court with other creators.

P27.content:
In this combination, also pay attention to actions such as:

- initiating an advantage and remaining a threat after passing;
- receiving an advantage created by someone else and preserving it with a quick decision;
- switching between handling and spacing without breaking the rhythm;
- influencing the possession without monopolizing the dribble.

Ask yourself whether you changed functions without the possession losing quality.

P04.content:
You do not need to develop every creation tool of a point guard and every possible form of small-forward versatility at the same time.

First build a simple connection between both functions:

- handle safely;
- pass on time;
- catch ready;
- attack a clear advantage;
- cut when the space calls for it;
- keep participating after giving up the ball.

Then expand the situations from which you can create or connect an advantage.

The combination gains value from adaptability, not from the number of tools accumulated.

P10.content:
Connect organization fundamentals with continuity fundamentals.

You need to be able to:

- handle and pass under reasonable pressure;
- catch ready;
- attack a closeout without needing too many dribbles;
- stop and pass again in balance;
- cut and relocate;
- finish a simple advantage;
- take over handling again if the possession requires it.

Do not train these tools as separate compartments.

The value appears when you move from one to another without the possession losing rhythm.

P27.content:
In this combination, also notice:

- initiating and then occupying a useful space;
- connecting two advantages with a simple pass;
- attacking a closeout without stopping the action;
- taking over organization when a possession breaks down.

Ask yourself whether your versatility simplified the possession.

P04.content:
Do not try to develop every perimeter creation tool and every interior solution at the same time.

Start by connecting simple actions:

- handle and pass safely;
- set a legal screen;
- roll into a useful space;
- catch and decide quickly;
- pass from the high post or short roll;
- re-enter the action after giving up the ball.

Then expand the reads and spaces from which you can create.

The combination gains value when its tools make one another better.

P27.content:
In this combination, notice especially:

- initiating an action and then becoming the screener;
- creating a second advantage from the short roll;
- connecting with the weak side from the high post;
- using a handoff without stopping the possession.

Ask yourself whether you created from several places without needing more of the ball.

P04.content:
Do not try to develop every perimeter playmaking tool and every interior solution at the same time.

Start by connecting simple actions:

- handle and pass safely;
- set legal screens;
- roll with intention;
- catch and protect;
- pivot and find a pass;
- make a quick decision from the short roll or high post;
- keep playing after giving up the ball.

Then expand the reads.

The combination gains value when you can organize from more than one space without losing simplicity.

P10.content:
This intersection needs to connect perimeter and interior fundamentals without training them as two separate games.

Work especially on the connections between:

- handling and passing;
- catching and pivoting;
- screening and rolling;
- short roll and passing;
- rebounding and the first pass;
- high post and handoff;
- interior catches and help reads.

The transition between fundamentals matters as much as each fundamental in isolation.

**rebound → get your eyes up → pass;**

**screen → catch → decide;**

**handle → pass → occupy a new space;**

**catch inside → draw help → find another advantage.**

The combination loses value if every tool needs a different possession to exist.

P27.content:
In this combination, notice especially:

- turning a rebound into a good first pass;
- organizing after screening or receiving on the short roll;
- connecting a cut from the high post;
- creating an advantage through a handoff without holding the ball.

Ask yourself whether you helped organize from more than one context.

P04.content:
You do not need to develop every possible scoring method and all of a small forward's versatility at the same time.

First build a common base:

- catch ready;
- make open shots;
- maintain spacing;
- attack clear closeouts;
- cut with intention;
- move the ball;
- defend;
- help finish the rebound.

Then add solutions when the defense takes away your first option.

The combination works best when you expand your game around a reliable threat, not when you try to turn every tool into a specialty.

P10.content:
Connect perimeter threat with fundamentals that let you keep playing after the first catch.

Work especially on:

- foot preparation;
- catch-and-shoot;
- oriented catches;
- attacking closeouts;
- one or two functional dribbles;
- the extra pass;
- passing after penetration;
- cuts;
- relocations;
- finishing;
- rebounding from perimeter positions;
- defense on and off the ball.

The goal is not to add dribbling for the sake of dribbling.

It is to make sure a defense that closes your shot has not also ended your usefulness.

P27.content:
In this combination, also notice:

- remaining a threat even when no shot appears;
- preserving an advantage after a closeout;
- creating space through movement without receiving;
- continuing to contribute through passing, defense or rebounding after the first action.

Ask yourself whether your game stayed alive when your initial option disappeared.

P04.content:
You do not need to develop every form of perimeter scoring and every solution of a mobile interior player at the same time.

First build a reliable connection among:

- open shooting;
- off-ball movement;
- legal screening;
- rolling;
- popping;
- attacking closeouts;
- a simple pass after an advantage.

Then expand the reads.

The combination works when each new tool improves the previous ones.

Not when it simply accumulates tools from two different positions.

P10.content:
Connect perimeter-threat fundamentals with screening and rolling fundamentals.

Work especially on:

- foot preparation;
- catch-and-shoot;
- attacking closeouts;
- passing after penetration;
- screen positioning;
- roll;
- pop;
- short roll;
- catching in intermediate spaces;
- one or two functional dribbles;
- passing after help arrives;
- finishing;
- rebounding;
- relocation.

Do not train shooting and screening as two separate identities.

The defense should have problems precisely because the same action can end in a pop, roll or pass.

P27.content:
In this combination, notice especially:

- setting a screen and remaining a threat by popping;
- rolling and freeing a shot for someone else;
- making a good decision from the short roll;
- moving a help defender because of your perimeter threat.

Ask yourself whether you alternated pop and roll well.

P04.content:
Do not try to develop every form of perimeter scoring and every interior solution at the same time.

First build a simple connection among:

- open shooting;
- off-ball movement;
- legal screening;
- rolling;
- interior catches;
- simple finishing;
- passing after help arrives;
- correct spacing.

Then expand the solutions.

The combination works when perimeter and interior threat reinforce each other.

Not when they look like two different games that never meet in the same possession.

P07.content:
This combination can face very different rebounding responsibilities depending on where it is when the shot goes up.

From outside, a long rebound or transition-balance responsibility may appear.

From an interior position, you may need to box out, attack the ball or keep an offensive rebound alive.

Do not decide according to your position label.

Decide according to:

- where you are;
- where your opponent is;
- who is protecting transition;
- what trajectory the shot may produce.

The combination's versatility also requires changing responsibility correctly when your location changes.

P10.content:
Connect perimeter-catching fundamentals with interior fundamentals.

Work especially on the transitions among:

- catch-and-shoot;
- cutting;
- screening;
- rolling;
- interior catches;
- pivots;
- finishing;
- passing after help arrives;
- perimeter relocation.

Do not train shooting and interior play as two separate identities.

For example:

**pop → catch → attack;**

**screen → roll → finish or pass;**

**catch inside → draw help → find outside;**

**cut → do not receive → create space again.**

The threat must survive the change of area.

P21.content:
This combination can open a lot of space for others if it understands when to move toward the rim and when to move away from it.

Do not occupy inside the space a drive needs.

Do not stay outside if an action needs depth, a screen or a cut.

You can:

- space the floor;
- screen;
- cut;
- roll;
- receive inside;
- find a perimeter pass;
- relocate again.

Your usefulness increases when your movement gives others more space, not when you simply change areas.

P27.content:
In this combination, also notice:

- spacing the floor and then creating depth with a cut;
- screening and drawing interior help;
- receiving inside and finding a perimeter pass;
- popping back out after an action near the rim.

Ask yourself whether your threat remained alive when you changed areas.

P04.content:
You do not need to develop every perimeter tool of a small forward and every interior solution of a power forward at the same time.

First build a common base:

- occupy space well;
- catch ready;
- make open shots;
- cut with intention;
- attack clear closeouts;
- set legal screens;
- roll afterwards;
- make simple passes;
- box out;
- defend with discipline.

Then expand the situations from which you can contribute.

Versatility begins by making several basic responsibilities reliable.

Not by accumulating moves.

P10.content:
This intersection needs fundamentals that work in different spaces.

Work especially on:

- oriented catches;
- open shooting;
- attacking closeouts;
- one or two functional dribbles;
- passing after penetration;
- cuts;
- screen positioning;
- roll and pop;
- catches in intermediate spaces;
- pivots;
- finishing;
- rebounding;
- defense on and off the ball.

Do not train “perimeter play” and “interior play” as unrelated compartments.

Also work on the transitions:

**pop → catch → attack;**

**screen → roll → decide;**

**cut → do not receive → occupy space again;**

**rebound → run → join the next action.**

P27.content:
In this combination, notice especially:

- switching among spacing, cutting and screening without losing activity;
- attacking a closeout and preserving the advantage;
- helping on the boards from different positions;
- moving from a first action to another useful responsibility.

Ask yourself whether your versatility made the team's game simpler.

P04.content:
You do not need to develop every small-forward tool and every center solution at the same time.

First build a base that works in both contexts:

- occupy space correctly;
- cut with intention;
- catch ready;
- finish simple advantages;
- set legal screens;
- roll afterwards;
- pass when help arrives;
- box out;
- provide defensive help;
- protect the rim when appropriate.

Then expand the contexts from which you can contribute.

The combination works when you can change responsibility without losing reliability.

P10.content:
This combination needs to connect perimeter and interior fundamentals.

Work especially on:

- oriented catches;
- open shooting;
- attacking closeouts;
- one or two functional dribbles;
- cuts;
- screening;
- rolling;
- pivots;
- ball protection;
- finishing with both hands;
- passing from interior positions;
- passing after help arrives;
- rebounding;
- defensive help;
- verticality near the rim.

Do not train these tools as two separate games.

Also work on their connections:

**pop → catch → attack;**

**cut → catch inside → finish or pass;**

**screen → roll → decide;**

**box out → run → occupy the next space.**

P22.content:
This combination can see the defense from very different perspectives.

From outside, you can detect cuts, screens and weak-side movement.

From interior positions, you can see drives, help, rebounding and threats near the rim.

Use both perspectives.

Communicate:

- screens;
- switches;
- help;
- recoveries;
- cuts;
- rebounding responsibilities;
- interior threats.

Changing areas also changes the information you can provide.

P27.content:
In this combination, also notice:

- moving from the perimeter to an interior threat through a cut or roll;
- boxing out from different locations;
- helping protect the rim and recovering afterwards;
- returning to a useful perimeter space after an interior action.

Ask yourself whether you changed areas without losing responsibility.

In this combination, notice especially:

- alternating roll, short roll and pop according to the defense;
- maintaining interior presence without closing a driving lane;
- connecting from the high post without losing rebounding responsibility;
- protecting the rim and then taking part in the next action.

Ask yourself whether you expanded your solutions without weakening your interior impact.
